---
// 测评文章 #7：平板（Tom's Guide / Wirecutter 结构）
// 数据来源（2026-11-24 核实）：Apple 官网 iPad 规格页、Samsung US 规格表、Lenovo PSREF 规格 PDF、
// Wirecutter 平板指南、GSMArena 实测评测、Notebookcheck / Android Police 对 Idea Tab Pro 的评测、9to5Toys 价格追踪
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Best Tablets for Notes, Netflix and the Occasional Spreadsheet — DealWillow"
  description="The best tablets in the US right now: Apple's 11-inch iPad for most people, the Galaxy Tab S11 if you want an AMOLED screen and a pen in the box, and the Lenovo Idea Tab Pro for a 12.7-inch screen on a budget."
>
  <main class="wrap article">
    <a class="crumb" href="/">&larr; All reviews</a>

    <div class="head">
      <span class="cat-tag">Tablets</span>
      <h1>Best Tablets for Notes, Netflix and the Occasional Spreadsheet</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated Nov 2026 · Prices checked Nov 24, 2026 · US models only</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lede">
      <p>
        <strong>For most people, the 11-inch Apple iPad (A16) is still the tablet to buy.</strong>
        Wirecutter has kept it as its top pick for years, and the 128GB base storage is finally big
        enough to live with. It costs $100 more than it did at launch — Apple raised US iPad prices
        on June 25, 2026 — but it is still the cheapest way into the best tablet app library there is.
        If you want a pen in the box and a screen that makes Netflix look like a TV, that's the
        <strong>Samsung Galaxy Tab S11</strong>, now $300 off its list price. And if you mostly want
        a big, sharp slab for split-screen notes and movies without spending iPad money, the
        <strong>Lenovo Idea Tab Pro</strong> gives you 12.7 inches for about $320.
      </p>
    </div>


    <h2 class="block-title">Everything we recommend</h2>
    <div class="picks">
      <a class="pick" href="#ipad">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best overall</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Best apps, longest useful life, 128GB base</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Apple iPad 11-inch (A16)</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$449 · Apple</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#tabs11">
        <span class="pick-tag alt">Best Android tablet</span>
        <span class="pick-role">120Hz AMOLED, S Pen included, IP68</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Samsung Galaxy Tab S11</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$699.99 · Amazon (list $999.99)</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#ideatab">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best big screen for the money</span>
        <span class="pick-role">12.7-inch 3K, 144Hz, pen in most bundles</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Lenovo Idea Tab Pro</span>
        <span class="pick-price">~$320 · Best Buy</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ 1. iPad ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="ipad">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best overall</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">1. Apple iPad 11-inch (A16)</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Display</span><b>11 in IPS LCD · 2360 × 1640 · 60Hz</b></div>
        <div><span>Brightness</span><b>500 nits rated · 466 nits measured (GSMArena)</b></div>
        <div><span>Chip / RAM</span><b>A16 · 6GB</b></div>
        <div><span>Storage</span><b>128GB / 256GB / 512GB · no microSD</b></div>
        <div><span>Stylus</span><b>Apple Pencil (USB-C) or 1st gen only</b></div>
        <div><span>Port</span><b>USB-C at USB 2.0 speeds (480Mb/s)</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery</span><b>28.93Wh · rated 10 hours</b></div>
        <div><span>Weight</span><b>1.05 lb (477 g)</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Wirecutter's top pick for the best all-around tablet, years running</li>
            <li>The deepest tablet app library — the part you actually notice every day</li>
            <li>128GB is now the base, double what the previous model started with</li>
            <li>A16 is quick enough for streaming, notes and mid-weight games</li>
            <li>Apple Pencil (USB-C) is only $79, and third-party keyboard cases are cheap</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>$449 now, up from $349 — the June 2026 price rise was $100</li>
            <li>No Apple Intelligence, and 6GB of RAM means that won't change</li>
            <li>60Hz, non-laminated screen that glares; GSMArena measured 896:1 contrast</li>
            <li>No Pencil Pro, no microSD, and USB-C is capped at USB 2.0 speeds</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The case for this iPad hasn't changed, even after the price rise: it's the only tablet
          where the software is the selling point. Wirecutter's writers have kept it as their top
          pick for years on the strength of the app library and the fact that it simply does
          everything a tablet is asked to do. At $449 for 128GB, you're paying about $100 more than
          the March 2025 launch price — Apple raised US iPad prices on June 25, 2026, citing memory
          and storage costs — but you also get double the base storage the previous generation shipped with.
        </p>
        <p>
          The screen is where the money was saved, and reviewers are unanimous about it. GSMArena's
          lab measured 466 nits against Apple's 500-nit claim, and a contrast ratio of 896:1 — fine
          indoors, washed out on a sunny patio. The panel is not laminated to the glass and has no
          anti-reflective coating, so it glares and feels slightly hollow under a stylus. PhoneArena
          was blunt about it and scored the tablet 6.4/10; TechRadar gave the same device 4.5/5. Both
          are describing the same hardware, which tells you how much the screen matters to you
          personally is the whole decision.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two limits worth knowing before you buy. There's no Apple Intelligence here — the A16 and
          6GB of RAM don't meet the bar, and that's a permanent limitation, not a pending update.
          And the USB-C port runs at USB 2.0 speeds, so moving video files onto it is slow; it's a
          charging-and-accessory port. If either one is a dealbreaker, the iPad Air (M4) at $749 is
          the step up, and it's the model Wirecutter recommends for daily work.
        </p>
        <p>
          On price, the sources disagree and it's worth checking before you click. Apple lists $449.
          CNET tracked the 128GB model at $427 on Amazon in recent weeks, while Wirecutter's buying
          links have shown $399 at Amazon — a figure that looks stale against Apple's current list.
          Treat anything under $430 as a good deal and confirm the exact color and storage at checkout.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> still the tablet most people should buy — just budget for a screen protector to kill the glare.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 2. Galaxy Tab S11 ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="tabs11">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best Android tablet</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">2. Samsung Galaxy Tab S11</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Display</span><b>11 in Dynamic AMOLED 2X · 2560 × 1600 · 120Hz</b></div>
        <div><span>Brightness</span><b>1,000 nits (HBM) · 1,600 nits peak, HDR</b></div>
        <div><span>Chip / RAM</span><b>MediaTek Dimensity 9400+ · 12GB</b></div>
        <div><span>Storage</span><b>128GB / 256GB / 512GB · microSD to 2TB</b></div>
        <div><span>Stylus</span><b>S Pen included (no Bluetooth)</b></div>
        <div><span>Durability</span><b>IP68, tablet and S Pen</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery</span><b>8,400mAh · 45W · up to 18h video</b></div>
        <div><span>Weight</span><b>1.03 lb (469 g) · 5.5mm thick</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>120Hz AMOLED at 11 inches — the best screen in this group by a distance</li>
            <li>S Pen in the box (a $79–$99 extra on the iPad), now with a hexagonal body</li>
            <li>IP68 on both tablet and pen; almost no other tablet offers that</li>
            <li>Seven OS upgrades and seven years of security patches from launch</li>
            <li>microSD to 2TB, so the 128GB model is genuinely viable</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>$999.99 list is iPad Air money; the current $699.99 is a promotion, not a new price</li>
            <li>No charger in the box — Wirecutter needed a 65W adapter and about 3 hours to full</li>
            <li>Keyboards are extra and run $80–$150</li>
            <li>Some owners report S Pen cursor offset and dead zones near magnets</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          Wirecutter's Android pick, and the reasoning is about value inside Samsung's own lineup:
          the Tab S11 carries the same core features and Galaxy AI tools as the pricier Tab S10+,
          with a newer chip, for less money. What you're buying over the iPad is hardware — an
          11-inch Dynamic AMOLED 2X panel at 2560 × 1600 and 120Hz, a Dimensity 9400+ with 12GB of
          RAM in every configuration, quad speakers, and a chassis 5.5mm thick at 1.03 lb.
        </p>
        <p>
          The S Pen ships in the box and Samsung redesigned it for this generation with a hexagonal
          body and a cone-shaped tip; Wirecutter's testers preferred it to the standard S Pen on the
          Tab S10 FE, and it attaches magnetically to the side. It has no Bluetooth, so you can't use
          it as a remote shutter or air-gesture controller — that's the one feature the redesign
          dropped. IP68 on both tablet and pen is genuinely rare and the reason this is the pick for
          a kitchen counter or a dorm room.
        </p>
        <p>
          Timing matters more here than on the other two. List price is $999.99 for 128GB, $1,099.99
          for 256GB and $1,299.99 for 512GB. In mid-November 2026, Amazon and Samsung both ran $300
          off as an early Black Friday promotion — $699.99, $799.99 and $999.99 — and 9to5Toys
          reported that as matching the lowest price tracked in three to four months. Best Buy has
          had open-box units for less. At $999.99 this is hard to justify against an iPad Air; at
          $699.99 it's the best Android tablet you can buy.
        </p>
        <p>
          The gripes are consistent across Samsung's own store reviews (4.7 stars, 194 ratings at the
          time of writing): value scores 4.7 but features and design sit in the 3.6–3.7 range, and AI
          functionality scores 2.0. Owners most often mention the missing charger and the missing
          extras, and one digital artist reported S Pen dead zones and cursor offset on multiple
          units — isolated enough that it's worth checking the pen inside your return window, not a
          reason to skip the tablet.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> buy it at $699.99, not at $999.99 — and add a charger and a keyboard case to the budget.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 3. Idea Tab Pro ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="ideatab">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best big screen for the money</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">3. Lenovo Idea Tab Pro (12.7-inch)</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Display</span><b>12.7 in LTPS LCD (3K) · 2944 × 1840 · 144Hz</b></div>
        <div><span>Brightness</span><b>400 nits rated · 409 nits measured (Notebookcheck)</b></div>
        <div><span>Chip / RAM</span><b>MediaTek Dimensity 8300 · 8GB</b></div>
        <div><span>Storage</span><b>128GB / 256GB · microSD to 1TB</b></div>
        <div><span>Stylus</span><b>Lenovo Tab Pen Plus (included in most bundles)</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery</span><b>10,200mAh · 45W</b></div>
        <div><span>Weight</span><b>1.37 lb (620 g) · 6.9mm</b></div>
        <div><span>Software</span><b>Android 14 at launch, upgrades to 16</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>A 12.7-inch 3K screen at 144Hz for around $320 — nothing else is close</li>
            <li>Notebookcheck rated it 82%; Android Authority gave it an Editor's Choice award</li>
            <li>Four JBL speakers with Dolby Atmos — loud enough to skip a soundbar</li>
            <li>microSD to 1TB and a pen in most bundles</li>
            <li>Notebookcheck measured 13h 18m of Wi-Fi browsing on one charge</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>400-nit screen is fine indoors and hopeless in direct sun</li>
            <li>620 g and 12.7 inches means two hands, always</li>
            <li>Software support is the weak point — and the sources disagree on how long it lasts</li>
            <li>It's now the previous generation; the Gen 2 is a different, pricier tablet</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          This is the value play, and the spec sheet explains why: 12.7 inches at 2944 × 1840, a
          144Hz refresh rate, four JBL speakers, a 10,200mAh battery, a microSD slot, and a pen in
          most bundles — for roughly $320 at Best Buy. Wirecutter calls it an affordable Android
          tablet with a large screen and notes the Dimensity 8300 is now two years old but still
          handled streaming and browsing fine. For split-screen notes or a movie on a tray table,
          the extra 1.7 inches over an 11-inch tablet is the whole point.
        </p>
        <p>
          The independent reviews land in the same place. Notebookcheck rated it 82% and measured
          409 nits against the 400-nit rating, plus 13 hours 18 minutes of Wi-Fi browsing. Android
          Police gave it 8.5/10 and Android Authority an Editor's Choice award, with both flagging
          weak viewing angles — an LCD problem, not a defect. Nobody is claiming this screen matches
          an AMOLED panel; it doesn't. It's simply very large and very sharp for the money.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two real trade-offs. First, brightness: at ~400 nits it looks good indoors and disappears
          in sunlight, so it's a couch-and-desk tablet, not a park-bench one. Second, support. Lenovo's
          own spec sheet says the tablet launched on Android 14 with Android 15 and 16 upgrades and
          four years of security patches; Wirecutter frames it as three years of OS updates and four
          years of patches; Notebookcheck and Tech Advisor say the last OS upgrade is Android 17, and
          other outlets put the patch window at 2028 rather than 2029. <em>Those figures could not
          be reconciled, so the conflict is flagged here rather than resolved in Lenovo's favour</em> —
          if you plan to keep a tablet for five years, that uncertainty is the reason to pay more for
          a Galaxy Tab.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also check the box. Lenovo's US spec sheet lists a 45W adapter as included, but the tablet
          has shipped in some regions and some SKUs without one, and reviewers note that ordinary
          USB-C chargers top it up very slowly. Confirm the bundle before checkout — and note that the
          Idea Tab Pro Gen 2 (13-inch, Snapdragon 8s Gen 4, launched March 2026) is a separate and
          more expensive tablet, not a refresh of this one.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the best large-screen tablet per dollar, as long as it never leaves the house.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Also worth knowing</h2>
      <p>
        If $449 is still too much, the <strong>Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 FE</strong> is the cheapest way
        to get IP68 and an S Pen in the box: $549.99 list, $469.99 in Samsung's current promotion, with
        a 10.9-inch 90Hz LCD, an Exynos 1380, 8GB of RAM and microSD support. If you want the iPad
        experience without the base model's screen, the <strong>iPad Air 11-inch (M4)</strong> at $749
        is Wirecutter's pick for daily work — laminated display, Apple Intelligence, Pencil Pro and
        Magic Keyboard support. And if you were waiting for an 11-inch Galaxy Tab S12, there isn't
        one: Samsung has launched the S12 Ultra and S12+ but kept the S11 as its small flagship,
        which is exactly why it's discounted right now.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">How we picked</h2>
      <p>
        The shortlist comes from Wirecutter's tablet guides and the models named in 2026 roundups from
        Notebookcheck, Android Police, GSMArena and CNET; every spec below was then checked against
        Apple's, Samsung's and Lenovo's own US spec pages. Prices were checked on
        <strong>Nov 24, 2026</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Before you buy: two things almost nobody tells you</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The stylus and keyboard are usually not in the box.</strong> Only Samsung bundles the
        S Pen; Apple's costs $79 (USB-C) or $99 (1st gen) and the Magic Keyboard Folio is $249, which
        is more than half the price of the tablet itself. A Galaxy Tab keyboard case runs $80–$150.
        Add those up before you compare stickers, because the gap between a $449 iPad and a $699
        Galaxy Tab can invert once accessories are in the cart.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Storage is soldered — but only two of these take a card.</strong> The iPad has no
        microSD slot, so whatever you buy is what you live with; 128GB is workable if you stream, tight
        if you download. Both Android picks take a card (2TB on the Galaxy Tab, 1TB on the Lenovo) for
        a fraction of what the storage upgrade costs, which makes the base model the smarter buy on
        those two.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

